{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async batching keeps the collection and trigger behavior described in the "},{"type":"link","href":"./batching.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Batching Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":", while adding Promise results, retries, error callbacks, failed-item tracking, and control over in-flight work."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use it when one async operation should process several collected items together. Use an "},{"type":"link","href":"./async-queuing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async Queue"}]},{"type":"text","value":" when each item needs its own execution or when you need to limit concurrency."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-async-batching-works","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How async batching works"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Items collect until any configured trigger fires:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"add A ─── add B ─── add C\n  │         │         │\n  └─ wait reset       └─ maxSize reached\n                            │\n                            └─ execute [A, B, C]","lang":"text"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A batch executes when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"its length reaches "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxSize"},{"type":"text","value":","}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getShouldExecute(items, batcher)"},{"type":"text","value":" returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", or"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"no new item arrives for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":" milliseconds."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxSize"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":" default to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Infinity"},{"type":"text","value":", so configure at least one trigger or call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":" manually. The wait timer restarts on every addition. It measures a quiet period, not a maximum age for the oldest item."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-an-api","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose an API"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectAsyncBatcher"},{"type":"text","value":" for flush, failed items, and selected state"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"angular-example","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Angular example"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { injectAsyncBatcher } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\nexport class AnalyticsComponent {\n  readonly batcher = injectAsyncBatcher(\n    sendEvents,\n    { maxSize: 20, wait: 1000 },\n    (state) => ({\n      size: state.size,\n      isExecuting: state.isExecuting,\n    }),\n  )\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The focused snippets later in this guide use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectAsyncBatcher"},{"type":"text","value":" and assume they run inside an Angular injection context."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"promise-results","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Promise results"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns the batch function's result and is the clearest way to await a specific batch."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addItem()"},{"type":"text","value":" also returns a Promise, but it should not be treated as an individual item's result receipt:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If that addition reaches "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxSize"},{"type":"text","value":" or satisfies "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getShouldExecute"},{"type":"text","value":", its Promise owns the triggered execution and resolves with the batch result."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If it only schedules the wait timer, its Promise resolves without the later batch result."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Another addition may reset the timer and change which items execute together."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSuccess"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSettled"},{"type":"text","value":" when all additions need to observe the eventual batch outcome."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"batch-boundaries-and-overlapping-work","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Batch boundaries and overlapping work"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The batcher copies and clears the current items before calling the async function. Items added while that function is active collect in a new batch:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"execute [A, B] ───────────────── finish\n       add C ─── add D ─── execute [C, D] ─── finish","lang":"text"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the second batch's trigger fires before the first finishes, both batch functions can overlap. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectAsyncBatcher"},{"type":"text","value":" does not have a concurrency option. Serialize batch executions outside the batcher or send the completed batches through an async queue when overlap is unsafe."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"errors-and-failed-items","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Errors and failed items"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async batchers provide these callbacks:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSuccess(result, batch, batcher)"},{"type":"text","value":" after success."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError(error, batch, batcher)"},{"type":"text","value":" after the batch's retries fail."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSettled(batch, batcher)"},{"type":"text","value":" after either outcome."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onItemsChange(batcher)"},{"type":"text","value":" when items are added or removed for execution."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":" or a size-triggering "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addItem()"},{"type":"text","value":" rejects on failure. Providing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":" changes that default to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":"; the Promise then resolves with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Items are removed from the pending collection before execution. A failed batch is not automatically requeued. Its items are added to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failedItems"},{"type":"text","value":" and are available through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"peekFailedItems()"},{"type":"text","value":" until "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clear()"},{"type":"text","value":" or a later execution clears that collection."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const failed = batcher.peekFailedItems()\nfor (const item of failed) {\n  saveForManualRecovery(item)\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For non-idempotent operations, verify the server outcome before resubmitting a failed batch."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"retrying-batches","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retrying batches"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure retries for each batch execution with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"asyncRetryerOptions"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const batcher = injectAsyncBatcher(sendEvents, {\n  maxSize: 20,\n  wait: 1000,\n  asyncRetryerOptions: {\n    maxAttempts: 3,\n    backoff: 'exponential',\n    baseWait: 500,\n    jitter: 0.2,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxAttempts"},{"type":"text","value":" includes the first attempt, and every retry receives the same copied batch. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./async-retrying.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async Retrying Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" before retrying operations with side effects."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"flushing-canceling-and-clearing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flushing, canceling, and clearing"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":" clears the pending timer and immediately executes the current items."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel()"},{"type":"text","value":" clears the pending timer but keeps the collected items."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clear()"},{"type":"text","value":" removes collected items and failed items, but does not clear a scheduled timer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"peekAllItems()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a copy of the currently collected items."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clear()"},{"type":"text","value":" leaves the timer in place, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancel()"},{"type":"text","value":" followed by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clear()"},{"type":"text","value":" when no empty timer should remain:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"batcher.cancel()\nbatcher.clear()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The batch function is not called when an eventual timer or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":" finds no items."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"aborting-active-work","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aborting active work"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"abort()"},{"type":"text","value":" aborts active retryers. It does not cancel a pending batch or remove collected items. Pass the batcher's signal to the underlying API for cancellation to propagate:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const batcher = injectAsyncBatcher(\n  async (events: Array<AnalyticsEvent>) => {\n    return fetch('/api/analytics/batch', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      body: JSON.stringify(events),\n      signal: batcher.getAbortSignal() ?? undefined,\n    })\n  },\n  { maxSize: 20, wait: 1000 },\n)\n\nbatcher.abort()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When executions overlap, pass an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"executionCount"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getAbortSignal()"},{"type":"text","value":" when you need a specific execution's signal."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"resetting-safely","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resetting safely"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" restores default state, but it does not clear a scheduled timer or guarantee that active underlying work stops. Use the lifecycle methods first when a complete cleanup is required:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"batcher.cancel()\nbatcher.abort()\nbatcher.reset()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"angular-lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Angular lifecycle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter cancels the pending wait timer and aborts active work when its owner is destroyed. Providing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUnmount"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces that default cleanup, so a custom callback must perform every required lifecycle action. When custom cleanup flushes work, remember that user callbacks can run while the component is being destroyed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuration-and-reactive-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration and reactive state"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter subscribes only to the state returned by the selector argument. Without a selector, the adapter state is empty. Create the utility in an Angular injection context, usually as a component or service field initializer and select only fields used by the view:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const batcher = injectAsyncBatcher(\n  sendEvents,\n  { maxSize: 20, wait: 1000 },\n  (state) => ({\n    size: state.size,\n    isExecuting: state.isExecuting,\n    failedItems: state.failedItems,\n  }),\n)\n\nconsole.log(\n  batcher.state().size,\n  batcher.state().isExecuting,\n  batcher.state().failedItems,\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option functions and lifecycle callbacks receive the underlying public utility instance. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".store.state"},{"type":"text","value":" reads inside those callbacks in the examples above are supported. Rendering code should read the selected adapter state shown here."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wait"},{"type":"text","value":" may be a number or a function that receives the batcher instance. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" merges new options, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"asyncBatcherOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" creates reusable, type-checked option objects."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"started"},{"type":"text","value":" to pause a batcher. It is currently a no-op, so every "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addItem()"},{"type":"text","value":" call evaluates the configured triggers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To restore selected state that your app has persisted, pass a partial snapshot through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":". It is merged with the defaults. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers and active executions are not restored."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Common state includes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"items"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"size"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPending"},{"type":"text","value":": The next batch and its timer state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isExecuting"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether a batch is reported as executing."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastResult"},{"type":"text","value":": The most recent successful result."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failedItems"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"totalItemsFailed"},{"type":"text","value":": Failure tracking."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"successCount"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"errorCount"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"settleCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Batch outcome counts."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"totalItemsProcessed"},{"type":"text","value":": Items in successful batch executions."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/index.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Angular API reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for adapter signatures and the public core reference for complete option and state types."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"how-async-batching-works","text":"How async batching works","level":2},{"id":"choose-an-api","text":"Choose an API","level":2},{"id":"angular-example","text":"Angular example","level":2},{"id":"promise-results","text":"Promise results","level":2},{"id":"batch-boundaries-and-overlapping-work","text":"Batch boundaries and overlapping work","level":2},{"id":"errors-and-failed-items","text":"Errors and failed items","level":2},{"id":"retrying-batches","text":"Retrying batches","level":2},{"id":"flushing-canceling-and-clearing","text":"Flushing, canceling, and clearing","level":2},{"id":"aborting-active-work","text":"Aborting active work","level":2},{"id":"resetting-safely","text":"Resetting safely","level":3},{"id":"angular-lifecycle","text":"Angular lifecycle","level":2},{"id":"configuration-and-reactive-state","text":"Configuration and reactive state","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nAsync batching keeps the collection and trigger behavior described in the [Batching Guide](./batching.md), while adding Promise results, retries, error callbacks, failed-item tracking, and control over in-flight work.\n\nUse it when one async operation should process several collected items together. Use an [Async Queue](./async-queuing.md) when each item needs its own execution or when you need to limit concurrency.\n\n## How async batching works\n\nItems collect until any configured trigger fires:\n\n```text\nadd A ─── add B ─── add C\n  │         │         │\n  └─ wait reset       └─ maxSize reached\n                            │\n                            └─ execute [A, B, C]\n```\n\nA batch executes when:\n\n- its length reaches `maxSize`,\n- `getShouldExecute(items, batcher)` returns `true`, or\n- no new item arrives for `wait` milliseconds.\n\nBoth `maxSize` and `wait` default to `Infinity`, so configure at least one trigger or call `flush()` manually. The wait timer restarts on every addition. It measures a quiet period, not a maximum age for the oldest item.\n\n## Choose an API\n\n- `injectAsyncBatcher` for flush, failed items, and selected state\n\n## Angular example\n\n```ts\nimport { injectAsyncBatcher } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\nexport class AnalyticsComponent {\n  readonly batcher = injectAsyncBatcher(\n    sendEvents,\n    { maxSize: 20, wait: 1000 },\n    (state) => ({\n      size: state.size,\n      isExecuting: state.isExecuting,\n    }),\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe focused snippets later in this guide use `injectAsyncBatcher` and assume they run inside an Angular injection context.\n\n## Promise results\n\n`flush()` returns the batch function's result and is the clearest way to await a specific batch.\n\n`addItem()` also returns a Promise, but it should not be treated as an individual item's result receipt:\n\n- If that addition reaches `maxSize` or satisfies `getShouldExecute`, its Promise owns the triggered execution and resolves with the batch result.\n- If it only schedules the wait timer, its Promise resolves without the later batch result.\n- Another addition may reset the timer and change which items execute together.\n\nUse `onSuccess`, `onError`, and `onSettled` when all additions need to observe the eventual batch outcome.\n\n## Batch boundaries and overlapping work\n\nThe batcher copies and clears the current items before calling the async function. Items added while that function is active collect in a new batch:\n\n```text\nexecute [A, B] ───────────────── finish\n       add C ─── add D ─── execute [C, D] ─── finish\n```\n\nIf the second batch's trigger fires before the first finishes, both batch functions can overlap. `injectAsyncBatcher` does not have a concurrency option. Serialize batch executions outside the batcher or send the completed batches through an async queue when overlap is unsafe.\n\n## Errors and failed items\n\nAsync batchers provide these callbacks:\n\n- `onSuccess(result, batch, batcher)` after success.\n- `onError(error, batch, batcher)` after the batch's retries fail.\n- `onSettled(batch, batcher)` after either outcome.\n- `onItemsChange(batcher)` when items are added or removed for execution.\n\nWithout `onError`, `throwOnError` defaults to `true`, so `flush()` or a size-triggering `addItem()` rejects on failure. Providing `onError` changes that default to `false`; the Promise then resolves with `undefined`.\n\nItems are removed from the pending collection before execution. A failed batch is not automatically requeued. Its items are added to `failedItems` and are available through `peekFailedItems()` until `clear()` or a later execution clears that collection.\n\n```ts\nconst failed = batcher.peekFailedItems()\nfor (const item of failed) {\n  saveForManualRecovery(item)\n}\n```\n\nFor non-idempotent operations, verify the server outcome before resubmitting a failed batch.\n\n## Retrying batches\n\nConfigure retries for each batch execution with `asyncRetryerOptions`:\n\n```ts\nconst batcher = injectAsyncBatcher(sendEvents, {\n  maxSize: 20,\n  wait: 1000,\n  asyncRetryerOptions: {\n    maxAttempts: 3,\n    backoff: 'exponential',\n    baseWait: 500,\n    jitter: 0.2,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`maxAttempts` includes the first attempt, and every retry receives the same copied batch. See the [Async Retrying Guide](./async-retrying.md) before retrying operations with side effects.\n\n## Flushing, canceling, and clearing\n\n- `flush()` clears the pending timer and immediately executes the current items.\n- `cancel()` clears the pending timer but keeps the collected items.\n- `clear()` removes collected items and failed items, but does not clear a scheduled timer.\n- `peekAllItems()` returns a copy of the currently collected items.\n\nBecause `clear()` leaves the timer in place, use `cancel()` followed by `clear()` when no empty timer should remain:\n\n```ts\nbatcher.cancel()\nbatcher.clear()\n```\n\nThe batch function is not called when an eventual timer or `flush()` finds no items.\n\n## Aborting active work\n\n`abort()` aborts active retryers. It does not cancel a pending batch or remove collected items. Pass the batcher's signal to the underlying API for cancellation to propagate:\n\n```ts\nconst batcher = injectAsyncBatcher(\n  async (events: Array<AnalyticsEvent>) => {\n    return fetch('/api/analytics/batch', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      body: JSON.stringify(events),\n      signal: batcher.getAbortSignal() ?? undefined,\n    })\n  },\n  { maxSize: 20, wait: 1000 },\n)\n\nbatcher.abort()\n```\n\nWhen executions overlap, pass an `executionCount` to `getAbortSignal()` when you need a specific execution's signal.\n\n### Resetting safely\n\n`reset()` restores default state, but it does not clear a scheduled timer or guarantee that active underlying work stops. Use the lifecycle methods first when a complete cleanup is required:\n\n```ts\nbatcher.cancel()\nbatcher.abort()\nbatcher.reset()\n```\n\n## Angular lifecycle\n\nThe adapter cancels the pending wait timer and aborts active work when its owner is destroyed. Providing `onUnmount` replaces that default cleanup, so a custom callback must perform every required lifecycle action. When custom cleanup flushes work, remember that user callbacks can run while the component is being destroyed.\n\n## Configuration and reactive state\n\nThe adapter subscribes only to the state returned by the selector argument. Without a selector, the adapter state is empty. Create the utility in an Angular injection context, usually as a component or service field initializer and select only fields used by the view:\n\n```ts\nconst batcher = injectAsyncBatcher(\n  sendEvents,\n  { maxSize: 20, wait: 1000 },\n  (state) => ({\n    size: state.size,\n    isExecuting: state.isExecuting,\n    failedItems: state.failedItems,\n  }),\n)\n\nconsole.log(\n  batcher.state().size,\n  batcher.state().isExecuting,\n  batcher.state().failedItems,\n)\n```\n\nOption functions and lifecycle callbacks receive the underlying public utility instance. The `.store.state` reads inside those callbacks in the examples above are supported. Rendering code should read the selected adapter state shown here.\n\n`wait` may be a number or a function that receives the batcher instance. `setOptions()` merges new options, and `asyncBatcherOptions()` creates reusable, type-checked option objects.\n\nDo not use `started` to pause a batcher. It is currently a no-op, so every `addItem()` call evaluates the configured triggers.\n\nTo restore selected state that your app has persisted, pass a partial snapshot through `initialState`. It is merged with the defaults. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers and active executions are not restored.\n\nCommon state includes:\n\n- `items`, `size`, and `isPending`: The next batch and its timer state.\n- `isExecuting`: Whether a batch is reported as executing.\n- `lastResult`: The most recent successful result.\n- `failedItems` and `totalItemsFailed`: Failure tracking.\n- `successCount`, `errorCount`, and `settleCount`: Batch outcome counts.\n- `totalItemsProcessed`: Items in successful batch executions.\n\nSee the [Angular API reference](../reference/index.md) for adapter signatures and the public core reference for complete option and state types.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Angular Async Batching Guide\nid: async-batching\n---\n\nAsync batching keeps the collection and trigger behavior described in the [Batching Guide](./batching.md), while adding Promise results, retries, error callbacks, failed-item tracking, and control over in-flight work.\n\nUse it when one async operation should process several collected items together. Use an [Async Queue](./async-queuing.md) when each item needs its own execution or when you need to limit concurrency.\n\n## How async batching works\n\nItems collect until any configured trigger fires:\n\n```text\nadd A ─── add B ─── add C\n  │         │         │\n  └─ wait reset       └─ maxSize reached\n                            │\n                            └─ execute [A, B, C]\n```\n\nA batch executes when:\n\n- its length reaches `maxSize`,\n- `getShouldExecute(items, batcher)` returns `true`, or\n- no new item arrives for `wait` milliseconds.\n\nBoth `maxSize` and `wait` default to `Infinity`, so configure at least one trigger or call `flush()` manually. The wait timer restarts on every addition. It measures a quiet period, not a maximum age for the oldest item.\n\n## Choose an API\n\n- `injectAsyncBatcher` for flush, failed items, and selected state\n\n## Angular example\n\n```ts\nimport { injectAsyncBatcher } from '@tanstack/angular-pacer'\n\nexport class AnalyticsComponent {\n  readonly batcher = injectAsyncBatcher(\n    sendEvents,\n    { maxSize: 20, wait: 1000 },\n    (state) => ({\n      size: state.size,\n      isExecuting: state.isExecuting,\n    }),\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe focused snippets later in this guide use `injectAsyncBatcher` and assume they run inside an Angular injection context.\n\n## Promise results\n\n`flush()` returns the batch function's result and is the clearest way to await a specific batch.\n\n`addItem()` also returns a Promise, but it should not be treated as an individual item's result receipt:\n\n- If that addition reaches `maxSize` or satisfies `getShouldExecute`, its Promise owns the triggered execution and resolves with the batch result.\n- If it only schedules the wait timer, its Promise resolves without the later batch result.\n- Another addition may reset the timer and change which items execute together.\n\nUse `onSuccess`, `onError`, and `onSettled` when all additions need to observe the eventual batch outcome.\n\n## Batch boundaries and overlapping work\n\nThe batcher copies and clears the current items before calling the async function. Items added while that function is active collect in a new batch:\n\n```text\nexecute [A, B] ───────────────── finish\n       add C ─── add D ─── execute [C, D] ─── finish\n```\n\nIf the second batch's trigger fires before the first finishes, both batch functions can overlap. `injectAsyncBatcher` does not have a concurrency option. Serialize batch executions outside the batcher or send the completed batches through an async queue when overlap is unsafe.\n\n## Errors and failed items\n\nAsync batchers provide these callbacks:\n\n- `onSuccess(result, batch, batcher)` after success.\n- `onError(error, batch, batcher)` after the batch's retries fail.\n- `onSettled(batch, batcher)` after either outcome.\n- `onItemsChange(batcher)` when items are added or removed for execution.\n\nWithout `onError`, `throwOnError` defaults to `true`, so `flush()` or a size-triggering `addItem()` rejects on failure. Providing `onError` changes that default to `false`; the Promise then resolves with `undefined`.\n\nItems are removed from the pending collection before execution. A failed batch is not automatically requeued. Its items are added to `failedItems` and are available through `peekFailedItems()` until `clear()` or a later execution clears that collection.\n\n```ts\nconst failed = batcher.peekFailedItems()\nfor (const item of failed) {\n  saveForManualRecovery(item)\n}\n```\n\nFor non-idempotent operations, verify the server outcome before resubmitting a failed batch.\n\n## Retrying batches\n\nConfigure retries for each batch execution with `asyncRetryerOptions`:\n\n```ts\nconst batcher = injectAsyncBatcher(sendEvents, {\n  maxSize: 20,\n  wait: 1000,\n  asyncRetryerOptions: {\n    maxAttempts: 3,\n    backoff: 'exponential',\n    baseWait: 500,\n    jitter: 0.2,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`maxAttempts` includes the first attempt, and every retry receives the same copied batch. See the [Async Retrying Guide](./async-retrying.md) before retrying operations with side effects.\n\n## Flushing, canceling, and clearing\n\n- `flush()` clears the pending timer and immediately executes the current items.\n- `cancel()` clears the pending timer but keeps the collected items.\n- `clear()` removes collected items and failed items, but does not clear a scheduled timer.\n- `peekAllItems()` returns a copy of the currently collected items.\n\nBecause `clear()` leaves the timer in place, use `cancel()` followed by `clear()` when no empty timer should remain:\n\n```ts\nbatcher.cancel()\nbatcher.clear()\n```\n\nThe batch function is not called when an eventual timer or `flush()` finds no items.\n\n## Aborting active work\n\n`abort()` aborts active retryers. It does not cancel a pending batch or remove collected items. Pass the batcher's signal to the underlying API for cancellation to propagate:\n\n```ts\nconst batcher = injectAsyncBatcher(\n  async (events: Array<AnalyticsEvent>) => {\n    return fetch('/api/analytics/batch', {\n      method: 'POST',\n      body: JSON.stringify(events),\n      signal: batcher.getAbortSignal() ?? undefined,\n    })\n  },\n  { maxSize: 20, wait: 1000 },\n)\n\nbatcher.abort()\n```\n\nWhen executions overlap, pass an `executionCount` to `getAbortSignal()` when you need a specific execution's signal.\n\n### Resetting safely\n\n`reset()` restores default state, but it does not clear a scheduled timer or guarantee that active underlying work stops. Use the lifecycle methods first when a complete cleanup is required:\n\n```ts\nbatcher.cancel()\nbatcher.abort()\nbatcher.reset()\n```\n\n## Angular lifecycle\n\nThe adapter cancels the pending wait timer and aborts active work when its owner is destroyed. Providing `onUnmount` replaces that default cleanup, so a custom callback must perform every required lifecycle action. When custom cleanup flushes work, remember that user callbacks can run while the component is being destroyed.\n\n## Configuration and reactive state\n\nThe adapter subscribes only to the state returned by the selector argument. Without a selector, the adapter state is empty. Create the utility in an Angular injection context, usually as a component or service field initializer and select only fields used by the view:\n\n```ts\nconst batcher = injectAsyncBatcher(\n  sendEvents,\n  { maxSize: 20, wait: 1000 },\n  (state) => ({\n    size: state.size,\n    isExecuting: state.isExecuting,\n    failedItems: state.failedItems,\n  }),\n)\n\nconsole.log(\n  batcher.state().size,\n  batcher.state().isExecuting,\n  batcher.state().failedItems,\n)\n```\n\nOption functions and lifecycle callbacks receive the underlying public utility instance. The `.store.state` reads inside those callbacks in the examples above are supported. Rendering code should read the selected adapter state shown here.\n\n`wait` may be a number or a function that receives the batcher instance. `setOptions()` merges new options, and `asyncBatcherOptions()` creates reusable, type-checked option objects.\n\nDo not use `started` to pause a batcher. It is currently a no-op, so every `addItem()` call evaluates the configured triggers.\n\nTo restore selected state that your app has persisted, pass a partial snapshot through `initialState`. It is merged with the defaults. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers and active executions are not restored.\n\nCommon state includes:\n\n- `items`, `size`, and `isPending`: The next batch and its timer state.\n- `isExecuting`: Whether a batch is reported as executing.\n- `lastResult`: The most recent successful result.\n- `failedItems` and `totalItemsFailed`: Failure tracking.\n- `successCount`, `errorCount`, and `settleCount`: Batch outcome counts.\n- `totalItemsProcessed`: Items in successful batch executions.\n\nSee the [Angular API reference](../reference/index.md) for adapter signatures and the public core reference for complete option and state types.\n"}
